#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas Stephen|chapter=The Rise, Progress, and Gradual Improvement of the Laws of England|title=The Book of the Constitution of Great Britain:{{nb...|Containing a Full Account of the Rise, Progress, and Present Construction of the Three Estates of the Realm, King, Lords, and Commons; of the Various Courts of Jurisdiction; and of Those Acts by which the Liberties or Rights of the Subject are Affected.}}|location=Glasgow; Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:Blackie and Son|Blackie & Son]],{{nb...|8, East Clyde Street, and 5, South College Street, Edinburgh}}; Dublin: W. Curry, Jun., & Co.; London: Simpkin & Marshall|year=1835|pages=275–276|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=o2YPAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA276|oclc=63002787|passage=After the first day of May, 1822, the bank of England, if they shall think fit, may pay or exchange the lawful coin of the realm, for any of their own notes, payable '''on demand'''.}}

